// Several functions under one name.
//
// A lexer, a parser and a type checker are all one enormous question: what kind of node is this,
// and what do I do about it? Written as a chain of type tests, that question is answered the same
// way in every pass, at length, and the chain has to be kept in step by hand.
//
// This program answers it by declaring several versions of a name and letting the call pick. Three
// things are worth watching:
//
//   - The version is chosen from what the value IS, not from what the call site knows. Everything
//     below walks a catalogue, so at the call site every node is one-of-three and nothing more.
//   - A version may add a `when`, and two conditions that could BOTH hold are refused rather than
//     ranked. `left is 0` alone would overlap `right is 0` on `0 + 0`, so the narrower one says so.
//   - Two arguments can tell versions apart, which is what a type checker actually needs: the
//     answer depends on the node AND on the type it is being checked against.

// ── The nodes, and the types they might be checked against ─────────────────────────────────────

Define object number-literal with (the number value).
Define object text-literal with (the text value).
Define object sum with (the number left, the number right).

// A type that carries nothing says so once — no `with ()`, and no `{ }` to make one.
Define object wants-number.
Define object wants-text.

// ── One argument: a version per node ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

Bind text to describe, given (the number-literal node):
    Return "the number {node's value}".
Done.

Bind text to describe, given (the text-literal node):
    Return "the text \"{node's value}\"".
Done.

Bind text to describe, given (the sum node):
    Return "{node's left} + {node's right}".
Done.

// ── A condition: constant folding ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// All three take a `sum`, so nothing about the TYPE tells them apart — the `when` does. The last
// one carries none, and runs when neither condition holds.
//
// ⚠ The first says `and node's right is not 0` for a reason: without it, `left is 0` and
// `right is 0` would both hold on `0 + 0`, and the language refuses two versions that can both
// apply rather than deciding which wins.

Bind number to fold, given (the sum node) when node's left is 0 and node's right is not 0:
    Return node's right.
Done.

Bind number to fold, given (the sum node) when node's right is 0:
    Return node's left.
Done.

Bind number to fold, given (the sum node):
    Return node's left + node's right.
Done.

// ── Two arguments: the node AND what it is checked against ─────────────────────────────────────
//
// Every combination needs a version. Three node types against two wanted types is six, and leaving
// one out is refused — a call could pass that pair, and nothing would run.

Bind text to check, given (the number-literal node, the wants-number want):
    Return "ok".
Done.

Bind text to check, given (the number-literal node, the wants-text want):
    Return "wanted text, found a number".
Done.

Bind text to check, given (the text-literal node, the wants-number want):
    Return "wanted number, found a text".
Done.

Bind text to check, given (the text-literal node, the wants-text want):
    Return "ok".
Done.

Bind text to check, given (the sum node, the wants-number want):
    Return "ok".
Done.

Bind text to check, given (the sum node, the wants-text want):
    Return "wanted text, found a sum".
Done.

// ── The same question, written by hand ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// What the versions above stand for. One `Judge` per argument, nested, with the outer subject held
// in a name before descending — `it` belongs to the nearest `Judge`, so the inner one takes it.
//
// The grouped arm is the part that reads better here than as versions: two node types share an
// answer, and saying so once is the point.

Bind text to shape-of, given (the (number-literal or text-literal or sum) node,
                              the (wants-number or wants-text) want):
    Judge node, where it is:
        A number-literal or a text-literal:
            Judge want, where it is:
                A wants-number, return "a leaf, against number".
                A wants-text, return "a leaf, against text".
            Done.
        Done.
        A sum:
            Define held as it.
            Judge want, where it is:
                A wants-number, return "a sum of {held's left}, against number".
                A wants-text, return "a sum of {held's left}, against text".
            Done.
        Done.
    Done.
Done.

// ── Walking them ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

Define nodes as a catalogue of (number-literal or text-literal or sum) with (
    a new number-literal { the value 42 },
    a new text-literal { the value "hello" },
    a new sum { the left 0, the right 9 },
    a new sum { the left 4, the right 0 },
    a new sum { the left 0, the right 0 },
    a new sum { the left 2, the right 3 }).

State "Describing each node:".
For each node in nodes, repeat:
    State "  {cast describe on (node)}".
Done.

State "".
State "Folding the sums:".
For each node in nodes, repeat:
    If node is a sum, state "  {cast describe on (node)} folds to {cast fold on (node)}".
Done.

State "".
State "Checking each node against 'number':".
Define wanted as a new wants-number.
For each node in nodes, repeat:
    State "  {cast describe on (node)} — {cast check on (node, wanted)}".
Done.

// The arguments may be named instead of ordered, and named ones may come in any order.
State "".
State "Checking the first node against 'text', by name:".
State "  {cast check on (the want a new wants-text, the node the first of nodes)}".

State "".
State "The hand-written pass, on the same nodes:".
For each node in nodes, repeat:
    State "  {cast shape-of on (node, wanted)}".
Done.
